# The Topological Charge of Knotted Light

Light can spiral around its own dark core like the steps of a corkscrew. The number of turns per lap is an integer — the **topological charge** — and this page lets you see, compute, and play with it.

## 1. What a vortex looks like

The field of an optical vortex is the phase ramp $\varphi_\ell(\theta) = e^{i\ell\theta}$. Its phase winds $\ell$ times around the central singularity. Drag the slider below to change the charge and watch the phase wheel spin.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The still image below shows the same phase wheel together with the path the field traces in the complex plane — it circles the origin exactly $\ell$ times.

{{visualization:0}}

## 2. Measuring the twist

The charge is the **winding number** $w(\varphi) = \frac{1}{2\pi i}\oint \varphi'/\varphi\,d\theta$. Two independent numerical estimators — phase unwrapping and contour integration — both return exactly $\ell$:

{{demo:0}}

The unwrapping estimator is beautifully simple; here it is in full.

{{algorithm:0}}

And the contour-integral estimator, which converges spectrally fast for smooth beams:

{{algorithm:1}}

How fast does the estimate lock onto the integer? This convergence plot answers that:

{{visualization:1}}

## 3. Why the charge is always an integer

Quantization is not assumed — it is forced by single-valuedness. Even a wobbly, amplitude-modulated loop still has an integer charge:

{{demo:1}}

You can watch the accumulated phase $G(\theta)$ climb and land exactly on $2\pi\ell$ at the end of one turn:

{{interactive_demo:2}}

## 4. Charge arithmetic

Multiplying beams **adds** charges; inverting **negates**; brightness does nothing; adding a beam to itself does **not** double the charge. Explore the algebra directly:

{{interactive_demo:1}}

Under the hood this is a signed sum — the additivity homomorphism in code:

{{algorithm:2}}

All of the algebraic laws in one run:

{{demo:2}}

## 5. Annihilation

Multiply a $+1$ vortex by a $-1$ vortex and the twist vanishes — the product is an ordinary, flat-phase beam of charge $0$:

{{visualization:2}}

That is the whole story: light ties itself in knots, and a single integral counts them, one whole turn at a time.
